[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Heaps: Binary Heaps, Priority Queues"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Heaps"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"A heap is a specialized tree-based data structure that satisfies the heap property: if A is the parent node of B, then the key (the value) of node A is ordered with respect to the key of node B with the same ordering applying across the heap. A heap can be one of two types: Min Heap or Max Heap."},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Min Heap: The key of each node is less than or equal to the key of its children. The minimum element is always at the root."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Max Heap: The key of each node is greater than or equal to the key of its children. The maximum element is always at the root."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Binary Heaps"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A binary heap is a heap data structure that takes the form of a binary tree. It is usually implemented as an array, which makes it an implicit data structure. In a binary heap, the tree is a complete binary tree (all levels are completely filled except possibly the last level, which is filled from left to right)."},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Given an element at index 'i' in the array representation:"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The left child is at index 2*i + 1."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The right child is at index 2*i + 2."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The parent is at index (i-1)/2 (integer division)."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Operations on Binary Heaps"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Insert: Add a new element to the heap. Place the element at the end of the array and then 'heapify up' to restore the heap property."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Extract Min/Max: Remove the root element (minimum in a Min Heap, maximum in a Max Heap). Replace the root with the last element in the array, reduce the size of the heap by one, and then 'heapify down' to restore the heap property."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Heapify Up: Move a node up in the tree until the heap property is satisfied. This is used after insertion."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Heapify Down: Move a node down in the tree until the heap property is satisfied. This is used after extracting the root."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Example of heapifyDown (Min Heap)
function heapifyDown(arr, n, i) {
 let smallest = i; // Initialize smallest as root
 let l = 2 * i + 1; // left = 2*i + 1
 let r = 2 * i + 2; // right = 2*i + 2

 // If left child is smaller than root
 if (l < n && arr[l] < arr[smallest])
 smallest = l;

 // If right child is smaller than smallest so far
 if (r < n && arr[r] < arr[smallest])
 smallest = r;

 // If smallest is not root
 if (smallest != i) {
 let swap = arr[i];
 arr[i] = arr[smallest];
 arr[smallest] = swap;

 // Recursively heapify the affected sub-tree
 heapifyDown(arr, n, smallest);
 }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Priority Queues"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"A priority queue is an abstract data type that is like a regular queue or stack data structure, but additionally, each element has a "priority" associated with it. In a priority queue, an element with high priority is served before an element with low priority. If two elements have the same priority, they are served according to their order in the queue."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Heaps are commonly used to implement priority queues because they provide efficient O(log n) time complexity for insertion and extraction of the highest/lowest priority element."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Priority Queue Operations"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Insert (enqueue): Add an element to the queue with a given priority."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Extract Max/Min (dequeue): Remove the element with the highest (or lowest) priority from the queue."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Peek: View the element with the highest (or lowest) priority without removing it."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation of Priority Queue using Binary Heap"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"A priority queue can be efficiently implemented using a binary heap. A Min Heap can be used to implement a priority queue where lower values represent higher priority, and a Max Heap can be used where higher values represent higher priority."},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"When inserting an element, add it to the heap and heapify up. When extracting the highest/lowest priority element, remove the root and heapify down."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Applications of Heaps and Priority Queues"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Heap Sort: An efficient sorting algorithm with O(n log n) time complexity."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dijkstra's Algorithm: Finding the shortest path in a graph."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Operating Systems: Scheduling processes based on priority."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Event-driven simulation: Managing events based on their scheduled time."}]
